    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **274 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented digits-only telephone storage with normalization applied before the uniqueness check.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Relaxed the `telephone` validation pattern from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[-0-9 ()]*$` so punctuated input like `(613) 555-0100` passes `@Valid` on the request DTO (the DTO is regenerated from this spec at build time). Updated the description to document the strip-on-input behavior.\n\n2. **`Telephones.java`** (new) \u2014 Small shared helper `digitsOnly(...)` that strips everything but digits, keeping the normalization rule in one place.\n\n3. **`BuildOwner.java`** (POST) \u2014 Strips the mapped telephone to digits before publishing the `Owner`, so the stored value \u2014 and thus the `telephone` field returned by GET \u2014 is digits only. This runs *before* the `ensureUnique` step in the pipeline.\n\n4. **`ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT) \u2014 Same digits-only stripping when updating an owner, keeping storage consistent.\n\n5. **`EnsureOwnerUnique.java`** \u2014 Telephone comparisons now use `normalizeTelephone(...)` (digit-stripping) on both the candidate and each existing owner, so `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide as duplicates even if any stored value were ever punctuated.\n\nBoth new Cp11 tests pass: `coreStoresDigitsOnly` (formatted input stored/returned as digits) and `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected` (punctuated duplicate \u2192 409), and no existing tests regressed.",
